ains that the engine sits  behind the front axle in combination  with the trans axle transmission meaning  that the transmission is in the rear  this makes for more dynamic performance  the Vantage flies from zero to 100  kilometres an hour and 4.8 seconds and  has a top speed of 305 kilometers an  hour Montes says the transmission allows  for short shifts but some muscle power  is needed to change gears the one thing  that bothers him about the gearbox is  that the car often jumps out of reverse  while backing up he says that shouldn't  happen in the car of this class and  Montes has another quibble - he's not so  impressed with the sat-nav from an Aston  Martin he expects something that's been  designed to suit the cars not just taken  over almost entirely from a firm like  Garmin and he finds the controls which  are placed down here behind the  gearshift to be really fiddly you can  only go up and down left and right not  turn and it's hard to get at them when  you're in an uneven gear which isn't so  practical but he says to be fair people  are unlikely to use a car like this for  long road trips where you really need a  Sat Nav and you can always retract it at  the push of a button  inside the quality of the decor and  workmanship are top-notch the mix of  materials which includes leather  synthetic suede metal and carbon fiber  is pleasing to the eye and montes thinks  the and 430 offers a fairly generous  amount of luggage space  there might not be enough room to  stretch out and back here in the trunk  but it's not bad for a sports car sports  body it's this Donnie's little snake the  trunk offers 190 litres of cargo space  the Vantage and 430 has a starting price  of 120 thousand euros in Germany our  test car cost just over one hundred  thirty thousand where their money buyers  get to drive one of the last sports cars  in its class powered by a naturally  aspirated engine  as our this not assess basically the  Vantage n 430 has everything he expects  from 